Chapter: CapWord64

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The capability word is 64 bits wide and the loader reads and writes it
 whole. Until 2026-09-02 it went through poke-32 and peek-32, so a
 capability at bit 32 or above was discarded at WRITE time and invisible at
 read time.

 The two magnitudes are the ones that separate the widths. Bit 31 is
 2147483648, the first value an ALU imm32 sign-extends under REX.W; bit 40
 is 1099511627776, the first band a 32-bit access cannot represent at all.

 The last row is the CONTROL and it is the point of the file: bit 40 driven
 through the 32-bit pair answers 0, because the low 32 bits of 2^40 are
 zero. A version of this chapter without that row would pass against a
 compiler that had quietly narrowed the qword pair back to 32 bits, since
 every other row here fits in 32 bits or is a value the narrow path happens
 to carry.

 The expected values are worked out by hand, not captured from a run. A
 sidecar captured from the subject enshrines whatever the subject does,
 which is how a printer defect nearly acquired a guard asserting it.

Section: Body

  bit31 : Integer = 2147483648

  bit40 : Integer = 1099511627776

  small : Integer = 305419896

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let a = alloc-bytes 64
    in let w1 = poke-qword a 0 bit31
    in let w2 = poke-qword a 8 bit40
    in let w3 = poke-qword a 16 small
    in let w4 = poke-32 a 24 small
    in let w5 = poke-32 a 32 bit40
    in act
      print-line-uni ("q-bit31 : " & show (peek-qword a 0))
      print-line-uni ("q-bit40 : " & show (peek-qword a 8))
      print-line-uni ("q-small : " & show (peek-qword a 16))
      print-line-uni ("d-small : " & show (peek-32 a 24))
      print-line-uni ("d-bit40 : " & show (peek-32 a 32))
    end
  end
